# CLAUDE.md — Frontend Website Rules

## Project: Misono
High-end Japanese omakase restaurant in Kenya (Nairobi assumed — unconfirmed).
Register is **quiet luxury**: dark, cinematic, intimate, editorial. Not a sushi website.

- Pages: `index.html` — hero + "A unique experience". Shared JS: `assets/site.js`.
- Concept: **one continuous film.** The hero is a single full-screen scene at a time —
  slicing → garnishing → serving → repeat. Never show two clips at once; an earlier
  side-by-side triptych version was rejected for exactly that. The change of scene dips
  *through darkness* rather than dissolving flatly: the outgoing scene blurs and drifts
  back while a `.dip` layer deepens over it, then lifts on the incoming one. That dip is
  what stops it reading as a slideshow.
- **The lockup and the nav are siblings of the stage, never children of a scene.** MISONO
  must stay fixed and fully opaque while the film changes behind it — verified at
  opacity 1 throughout. Both sit above `.dip`. Do not move either inside `.stage`.
- **Type is chrome-scale, not headline-scale.** The footage is the hero; the wordmark
  sits quietly inside it ringed by negative space. Measured targets: wordmark **66px at
  1440 / 69px at 1920** (never above ~70), tagline **9px uppercase** at `.42em` tracking,
  nav links **10.4px uppercase** at `.26em`. Do not let the wordmark grow into a
  heading — an earlier version at 9.5vw was rejected for exactly that.
- Nav: the mon on the left (28px), `HOME / ABOUT / MENU / CONTACT` on the right, thin and
  wide-tracked. It was deliberately stepped up from 9px/22px to be easier to notice while
  still reading as chrome — keep it below the wordmark in weight. The mon lives **only**
  in the nav; the centre is wordmark + tagline alone, matching the reference. On phones
  the links drop back to 9px so four items never crowd the mark.
- Brand — **indigo (ai / aizome), derived from the logo's navy.** The supplied logo is a
  photo of print, so the navy was read by white-balancing the paper to `#FEFEFE` and
  sampling: `#3A5280`. **Still provisional** — replace with real brand values when a
  style guide or clean file arrives.
  `#080D17` ai (base, near-black indigo), `#101827` kon (elevated surface), `#3A5280`
  gunjō (the logo navy), `#EDE7DC` washi (primary type — never `#FFF`),
  `#A9A093` shirogane (type over footage), `#8A8177` hai (micro-labels).
  **No gold, no pure black, no bright white.**
- The veil colour is a bare triplet, `--veil:8 13 23`, consumed as
  `rgb(var(--veil) / .58)`. Every scrim stop reads from it — retune the palette there,
  not in individual rules.
- **Grade at encode time, not at runtime.** Each clip is darkened with ffmpeg `eq` +
  `curves` before it ships, so the CSS `.veil` only has to shape the frame. Full-screen
  CSS `filter` on video is expensive, and stacking a heavy veil on top of an already-dark
  clip turns the food to mud — which the brief explicitly forbids. If a scene reads wrong,
  fix its bake, not the veil.
- The stage is cool and the footage is graded warm. That warm-food-on-cool-indigo tension
  is deliberate; don't neutralise one side of it.
- Type: Marcellus (wordmark only; inscriptional roman, flared serifs echo brush terminals)
  + Jost 300 (tagline, nav, all micro-labels), Google Fonts. Deliberately not Cormorant or
  Playfair — those are the default AI-luxury serifs.
- Assets: `brand_assets/misono logo.jpeg` is a **photo of the printed logo** — angled,
  soft, opaque paper background, so it cannot be placed in the hero as-is. The mon was
  traced from it into `misono-mark.svg` (on navy, used as the favicon) and
  `misono-mark-mono.svg` (transparent, drawn in the hero via CSS `mask` so its colour
  comes from the tokens — an `<img>` would break `currentColor`). **Both are
  reconstructions: verify against real artwork before shipping.** The wordmark is still
  typographic Marcellus, with a documented swap point in `index.html`.
  `assets/video/hero-{1-slice,2-plate,3-serve}[-tall].mp4` are **Pexels stock standins**,
  cropped and graded to fit — replace with real Misono footage, keeping the filenames.
  `assets/img/hero-1-slice[-tall].jpg` is scene 1's still, the only image visible before
  video paints. URL-encode any spaces in filenames.
- **H.264 MP4 only, no WebM.** VP9 was measured *larger* than H.264 on every one of these
  clips (dark footage with heavy film grain), so a second codec cost bytes and bought no
  reach. Don't reintroduce WebM without re-measuring.
- Motion: CSS only, easing `cubic-bezier(.16,1,.3,1)`. Entrance floats the wordmark up
  while opening its tracking (1400ms), tagline lagging 340ms. Scene timing lives in
  `--hold` / `--trans` on `:root` and `site.js` reads it back off the DOM, so the CSS
  transition and the sequencer can never disagree. Under `navigator.webdriver` or reduced
  motion, `site.js` sets `.no-anim`, never starts the sequence, and loads **no video at
  all** — scene 1's still is the static hero. `?anim=1` overrides this, and is the only
  way to see the sequence in a capture.
- Breakpoints: **≥768px** gets the 16:9 clips, below gets the 9:16 `-tall` crops. The
  `matchMedia` in `site.js` must stay in step with the CSS breakpoint or a viewport
  downloads the wrong crop.
- `assets/video/Design hero vid inspo.mp4` and `assets/img/design inspirtation 2page.jpeg`
  are the user's reference material, not site assets. Both sit in served directories —
  move them out before deploying, along with the ungraded `assets/img/food*.jpeg`
  originals, which only `bake-gallery.sh` needs.
- Placeholders pending real content: logo + wordmark, real Misono footage and stills,
  street address, phone, email, social links, menu, service hours, reservation link,
  and confirmation that the city is Nairobi.

## Section 2 — "A unique experience" (`.beyond`, `id="beyond"`)
Built from a supplied reference (`assets/img/design inspirtation 2page.jpeg`): centred
heading + small paragraph, then two rows of dishes shearing past each other, top row
right→left, bottom row left→right, with EXPLORE MENU sitting in the seam. It is the
hero's film continued as a strip — the edges dissolve into the indigo so it reads as one
gallery, not a row of cards. **No rounded corners, no shadows, no borders on the tiles.**
- **The whole composition must be readable in one screen.** The section is
  `min-height:100svh`, a centred flex column, and **every vertical measure is in `vh`,
  not `vw`** — that is what makes it fit at 720, 900 and 1080 alike instead of only at
  the width it was tuned on. Verified fitting exactly 100vh from 600px to 1300px tall.
  Keep it `min-height`: with `height` a viewport too short for the content would clip
  the heading off the top instead of letting the section grow.
- **Type stays under the hero.** Heading is Marcellus caps at **44px / 1440** (`.22em`) —
  deliberately below the wordmark's 66px, because MISONO must stay the largest thing on
  the page. Paragraph 13px Jost 300, `max-width:32.5rem` so it breaks on its own full
  stop. CTA 11px at `.28em` — a step above the 10.4px nav, well below the heading.
- **The CTA is clear at rest and fills ivory on hover** — a hairline frame with the film
  running through it, so the one piece of chrome crossing the strip does not break its
  continuity. That flip is the entire hover; it had an expanding ring too, which was cut
  as a second gesture doing the first one's job. Because there is no fill at rest, the
  label's legibility rests **entirely on `.gal-pool`** — measured 7.32:1 against the
  brightest pixel that passes behind it at any phase of the loop (mean 14:1). Weaken the
  pool and you silently break the button; re-measure if you retune it. `:active` repeats
  the fill because touch never reliably fires `:hover`.
- **The loop is pure CSS and must stay seamless.** Each track holds the nine dishes
  **three** times and travels `calc(-100% / 3)`, i.e. exactly one copy. Two traps, both
  paid for already:
  - Tiles use `margin-right`, **not** a flex `gap`. With `gap` the trailing edge of each
    copy is half a gap short and the wrap visibly jumps.
  - Three copies, not two. Tile height caps at 280px, so one copy is ~2385px — narrower
    than a 2560px monitor. Three copies carry it to 4764px. Verified by measurement.
- **Tile height carries a width term for a reason.** `clamp(max(7rem,6.3vw),28vh,21rem)`.
  A copy of the sequence is ~8.2× the tile height and two copies must span the viewport
  or the loop opens a visible gap. On a wide, short window `28vh` alone shrinks the tiles
  past that point, so the `6.3vw` floor holds them up. The trade is that a ~5:1 squashed
  window (3440×700) makes the section ~10px taller than the viewport — a 10px scroll is
  the right price for never showing a hole in the strip. Don't drop the `max()`.
- **Speed is set by relative motion, not seconds.** A copy's width scales exactly with
  tile height, so an unchanged duration already gives identical relative speed at every
  size. Mobile is nudged 40s/50s (vs 56s/70s) only so the drift does not read as
  stalled — an earlier 26s/32s made phones drift 2.2× faster than desktop.
- **Mobile is tuned on its own terms, not scaled down.** A phone spends far more of its
  height on the heading (two lines) and paragraph (three), so tiles take `24vh` rather
  than the desktop's `28vh`; otherwise the type and the strip fight for the same space.
- **Never put `decoding="async"` on the tiles.** It lets the browser present frames
  without them, so the strip renders with holes on first scroll (and blank in captures).
- Rows carry one `role="img"` + `aria-label` with `alt=""` on all 54 tiles — announcing
  three copies of nine dishes is noise. The CTA sits **outside** that element, or
  `role="img"` would make it presentational.
- `bake-gallery.sh` regenerates `assets/img/gallery/` from the originals in
  `assets/img/food*.jpeg`. Same rule as the hero: **grade at encode time.** It is a two
  pass grade — the nine sources are phone snapshots under different light (food3
  tungsten, food6 window-cool, food9 amber), so each is measured and white-balanced
  toward grey at 55% strength before one shared look goes on top. Judge changes with the
  numbers it prints (`YAVG` spread, `U`/`V` near 128), not by eye. One more trap: inside
  a single `curves`, `all` alongside `r`/`b` starves green and turns neutrals magenta —
  chain a second `curves` instead.
- Replacing these with real photography: keep the filenames, drop them in `assets/img/`,
  re-run `bake-gallery.sh`, and update the `width`/`height` attributes in `index.html`
  (they hold the track's layout before the images load).
- **Its grade no longer matches the page.** Sections 3–5 were built to a much deeper
  register and the strip is now the only bright thing on the site — white plates and
  vivid garnish against four dark sections. Fixing it means re-running `bake-gallery.sh`
  nearer the scenes' numbers (they land at `YAVG` 12–16); it is a grade change only, no
  layout. Left alone because section 2 was explicitly out of scope. **Ask before doing it.**

## Sections 3–5 and the footer (`.omakase`, `.craft`, `.reserve`, `.foot`)
Built from `assets/video/design pages vid.mp4`, studied frame by frame at 5fps. The film
continued as still frames: each section is one lit form in a dark room.
- **The head pattern is section 2's, shared by adding selectors to its rules** —
  `.beyond-title, .sec-title { … }` — never by copying the declarations. `.beyond`
  renders unchanged and the two can never drift. `.gal-cta` was split so the hairline box
  is shared but its absolute centring is not.
- **Measure the reference as a share of viewport width, not in pixels.** The recording is
  1024×576 of an ~1890px viewport (scale ×1.875; the form column is 410f = 768 real).
  Its headings look larger only because the viewport is larger and its wordmark is a big
  script. Against its own wordmark, Misono's 44px section title is already proportionally
  *bigger* — so the title size was measured, then deliberately left alone. Do not chase
  its absolute px.
- What measuring *did* change: the copy in these sections is 12px/1.62 at `27.5rem`,
  against section 2's 13px/1.95 at `32.5rem`. The reference sets a compact block under
  the heading (29.3% of viewport wide, 1.29% line pitch); this page was setting 35.4% at
  1.76%. Section 2's own values are pinned and stay.
- **Reveal: 8px rise, and the tracking opens `.20em → .22em`.** Both measured — its
  headings rise ~5.6px while brightening from a third to full, and the line widens 2.4%.
  The gesture is mostly a fade. An 18px slide read as a different, busier animation.
  The tracking open is the hero's `rise-word` move at chrome scale, which is what ties
  the halves of the page together; it is the one place a layout property is animated.
- **`.no-anim` renders these finished**, unlike the hero, whose static state is scene 1's
  still. These sections have no meaningful "before", and captures must show the real
  composition. The hidden state is guarded by `@media (scripting: enabled)`, not a class
  from `site.js` — the script is deferred, so a class arrives after first paint and the
  content flashes in before hiding.
- **`data-parallax` is a fraction of `--bleed`, not a pixel distance**, and `site.js`
  reads `--bleed` off the DOM — same reason the sequencer reads `--hold`. Hard-coding
  both let a later `--bleed` change leave them disagreeing, and the disagreement shows as
  the photograph's own edge sliding into the section. At `1` the frame travels exactly to
  its bleed. Verified at every breakpoint.
- **Bake the shape of the hole.** A scene's aspect must match the box it lands in — the
  section plus twice `--bleed` — because `object-fit:cover` discards the rest. `.craft`'s
  box is portrait even on a desktop; baked landscape, cover cropped it to the middle two
  thirds and dropped half the subject off the sides.
- **Feather top and bottom only, never the sides.** The reference's frames run clean off
  all four edges; a 12–15% side feather put a dark oval around every section and read as
  a filter. `--fade-top`/`--fade-bot` default to 4.5%. `.craft` needs far more (17%/20%)
  because it begins mid-section and the slate's ground is a warmer dark than the page's
  indigo — at 4.5% the join showed as a band across the section.
- **No `loading="lazy"` on the scene images.** Same trap as `decoding="async"` on the
  gallery tiles: the browser presents the page without them and the sections render empty
  in captures. Measured cost of eager: ~200KB.
- Section 4's frame is **not** repeated at the top of section 5. The reference bleeds its
  photograph over that join, but a second copy faded in from the top of `.reserve` put a
  hard horizontal edge across it — the two crops do not line up. `.craft`'s long foot
  fade carries the join instead. Judge that join by measuring row-mean luminance, not by
  eye: JPEG artefacts in a downscaled crop look exactly like a seam.
- **Both photographs are one shoot** — the same salmon loin, slate and light
  (`scene-omakase-src.jpeg`, `scene-craft-src.jpeg`, Pexels 3296276 / 3296280). That is
  the point: two unrelated stock frames read as two stock frames. The crops are
  deliberately unalike — a still form, then two hands and a blade — so the shared source
  buys cohesion without repeating a picture. They share one grade (`$LOOK`) and land
  within `YAVG` 12–16, `YMAX` ~175. `bake-scenes.sh` regenerates all four files.
- Form fields are square, filled `color-mix(in srgb, var(--kon) 52%, var(--ai))` — full
  `--kon` read as a row of blue-grey boxes. Focus moves the border to `--gunjo`. The
  select chevron is drawn in CSS and the date field is `type="text"`: a native picker
  arrives in another operating system's design language.
- The footer wordmark is **the hero lockup enlarged** — same uppercase, same `--track` —
  at 6% and clipped by the footer box. Set in mixed case it read as a second wordmark and
  the tittle on its "i" landed beside the nav looking like a stray dot.
- Placeholder pending real content: the reservation endpoint (`<form>` has no `action`).
- `assets/img/scene-*-src.jpeg` are ungraded originals that only `bake-scenes.sh` needs —
  move them out before deploying, with `assets/img/food*.jpeg` and the two reference files.

## Always Do First
- **Invoke the `frontend-design` skill** before writing any frontend code, every session, no exceptions.

## Reference Images
- If a reference image is provided: match layout, spacing, typography, and color exactly. Swap in placeholder content (images via `https://placehold.co/`, generic copy). Do not improve or add to the design.
- If no reference image: design from scratch with high craft (see guardrails below).
- Screenshot your output, compare against reference, fix mismatches, re-screenshot. Do at least 2 comparison rounds. Stop only when no visible differences remain or user says so.

## Brand Assets
- Always check the `brand_assets/` folder before designing. It may contain logos, color guides, style guides, or images.
- If assets exist there, use them. Do not use placeholders where real assets are available.
- If a logo is present, use it. If a color palette is defined, use those exact values — do not invent brand colors.

## Local Server
- **Always serve on localhost** — never screenshot a `file:///` URL.
- Start the dev server: `node serve.mjs` (serves the project root at `http://localhost:3000`).
- `serve.mjs` lives in the project root. Start it in the background before taking any screenshots.
- If the server is already running, do not start a second instance.
- `serve.mjs` refuses to bind an occupied port and says so. Other projects on this machine also use 3000 — if it is taken, run `PORT=3001 node serve.mjs` and screenshot `http://localhost:3001`. Check the served `<title>` matches this project before trusting a screenshot.

## Screenshot Workflow
- Puppeteer is installed locally in the project (`node_modules/`). Chrome cache is at `~/.cache/puppeteer/`.
- **Always screenshot from localhost:** `node screenshot.mjs http://localhost:3000`
- Screenshots save automatically to `./temporary screenshots/screenshot-N.png` (auto-incremented, never overwritten).
- Optional label suffix: `node screenshot.mjs http://localhost:3000 label` → saves as `screenshot-N-label.png`.
- Optional viewport (third arg): `node screenshot.mjs http://localhost:3000 label mobile` → `desktop` (default, 1440x900), `tablet` (820x1180), `mobile` (390x844), or `WIDTHxHEIGHT`.
- `screenshot.mjs` lives in the project root. Use it as-is.
- After screenshotting, read the PNG from `temporary screenshots/` with the Read tool — Claude can see and analyze the image directly.
- When comparing, be specific: "heading is 32px but reference shows ~24px", "card gap is 16px but should be 24px".
- Check: spacing/padding, font size/weight/line-height, colors (exact hex), alignment, border-radius, shadows, image sizing.
- **Clean up when done:** at the end of every assignment, delete the contents of `temporary screenshots/` (`rm -f "temporary screenshots/"*.png`). The PNGs are large and re-reading stale ones wastes tokens — regenerate fresh next time.

## Output Defaults
- Single `index.html` file, all styles inline, unless user says otherwise. (JS is the documented exception: motion lives in `assets/site.js`.)
- Tailwind CSS via CDN: `<script src="https://cdn.tailwindcss.com"></script>`
  - **The hero deliberately does not use it.** The Play CDN ships ~400KB of JS that
    compiles styles at runtime, and the hero brief made load speed the primary
    requirement. The hero is also almost entirely mask gradients, per-panel filters and
    keyframes — things utilities cannot express — so Tailwind would have added the weight
    and bought nothing. Total hero transfer is 644KB desktop / 283KB mobile. Reinstate
    Tailwind for ordinary content sections below the hero if you want it; do not add it
    to the hero without re-measuring.
- Placeholder images: `https://placehold.co/WIDTHxHEIGHT`
- Mobile-first responsive.

## Anti-Generic Guardrails
- **Colors:** Never use default Tailwind palette (indigo-500, blue-600, etc.). Pick a custom brand color and derive from it.
- **Shadows:** Never use flat `shadow-md`. Use layered, color-tinted shadows with low opacity.
- **Typography:** Never use the same font for headings and body. Pair a display/serif with a clean sans. Tight tracking (`-0.03em`) on large headings, generous line-height (`1.7`) on body.
- **Gradients:** Layer multiple radial gradients. Add grain/texture via SVG noise filter for depth.
- **Animations:** Only animate `transform` and `opacity`. Never `transition-all`. Use spring-style easing.
- **Interactive states:** Every clickable element needs hover, focus-visible, and active states. No exceptions.
- **Images:** Add a gradient overlay (`bg-gradient-to-t from-black/60`) and a color treatment layer with `mix-blend-multiply`.
- **Spacing:** Use intentional, consistent spacing tokens — not random Tailwind steps.
- **Depth:** Surfaces should have a layering system (base → elevated → floating), not all sit at the same z-plane.

## Hard Rules
- Do not add sections, features, or content not in the reference.
- Do not "improve" a reference design — match it.
- Do not stop after one screenshot pass.
- Do not use `transition-all`.
- Do not use default Tailwind blue/indigo as the primary color.
